.TH CCOLLECTIONS 7 "2026-10-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Overview"
.SH NAME
ccollections \- library-wide conventions of c_collections: return codes, fatal
errors, custom allocators, timeouts, thread safety and fork()
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/common.h>

typedef enum { ... } ccol_retval_t;

typedef struct ccol_memmgmt_procs_t {
  ccol_malloc_t  malloc;
  ccol_free_t    free;
  ccol_calloc_t  calloc;
  ccol_realloc_t realloc;
} ccol_memmgmt_procs_t;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
All the other public headers include common.h, which holds the types and
the rules that all the modules of the library share. This page gives
those rules once, and the page of each function gives only the
differences.
.fi
.SS "Two layers: typed macros and raw functions"
.nf
Each container module has two layers:
  - The type-inferred macros, such as chmap_insert(3) and cvec_push(3),
    take values of the declared types and call ccol_fatal_err() when an
    operation fails.
  - The raw functions below them, such as chmap_insert_elem(3), take
    cmap_pair arguments and give a ccol_retval_t. A raw function never
    stops the process on a failure that it can report.
Use the raw layer when a failure is an expected result that the caller
handles.
.fi
.SS "Return codes"
.nf
ccol_retval_t is zero for success and negative for a failure. Each
enumerator has an explicit value, and the values do not change within
one major version, so a comparison with 0 is as correct as a comparison
with ccol_success:

  ccol_success                             0
  ccol_not_enough_memory                  -1
  ccol_key_already_present                -2
  ccol_key_not_found                      -3
  ccol_invalid_args                       -4
  ccol_not_permitted                      -5
  ccol_timed_out                          -6
  ccol_container_full                     -7
  ccol_container_empty                    -8
  ccol_msg_too_large                      -9
  ccol_http_transfer_aborted             -10
  ccol_http_invalid_url                  -11
  ccol_http_too_many_redirects           -12
  ccol_http_tls_cert_load_failed         -13
  ccol_http_tls_cert_verification_failed -14
  ccol_http_tls_handshake_failed         -15
  ccol_http_host_resolution_failed       -16
  ccol_http_connection_failed            -17
  ccol_unexpected_failure                -18

The page of each function gives the codes that the function can
return, and what each code means for that function.
ccol_retval_to_str(3) gives the name of a code as a string.
.fi
.SS "Fatal errors"
.nf
A programming error is fatal. Examples of programming errors are:
  - a NULL where the call needs a valid handle
  - an index that is out of bounds
  - a stale handle
  - a missing key given to a _get macro.
A failure inside a typed macro, such as a failed allocation in
chmap_insert(3), is also fatal. In these cases the library calls
ccol_fatal_err(), which writes two lines to stderr in this form:

  <file>:<line>: fatal: <message>
  <file>:<line>: ccol_assert failed: false

It then stops the process with abort(3), which raises SIGABRT. Neither
NDEBUG nor any other compilation setting changes this: the checks are
part of the contract, and the compiler never removes them. Before the
fatal line, the map macros also write a hex dump of the key that
caused the failure.
.fi
.SS "Custom memory management"
.nf
Each module that allocates memory accepts a ccol_memmgmt_procs_t * when
it creates an object, through the *_mp, *_with_mprocs or *_full form of
the constructor; for cmempool, it is an argument of the constructor
itself. A NULL pointer selects the standard malloc(3), calloc(3),
realloc(3) and free(3), and so does a form of the constructor that
takes no table.

A table that is not NULL must hold four functions that are not NULL,
and each function must operate exactly as its standard counterpart,
alignment included: each block that malloc, calloc and realloc return
must have the alignment for any object type. That alignment is
_Alignof(max_align_t), which is 16 bytes on x86-64 and aarch64. The
containers put entries that need that alignment at the start of a
block, so with a block of smaller alignment each access to such an
entry is undefined behavior. An arena or pool allocator can give
smaller blocks, so such an allocator must round each block up.

An object frees all its memory through the same table, so a destroy
needs no allocator argument. The functions must stay valid until the
last call to them; for all the modules except cmempool, that last call
occurs before the destroy of the object returns. Also keep the
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t struct valid until that destroy, unless the object
keeps its own copy of the struct, which the page of its constructor
tells you. clrucache, cjson and cyaml keep a copy, so for them you can
release the struct after the creating call returns.

A ccol_mempool or ccol_r_mempool that threads share is an exception:
its allocator can run after ccol_mempool_destroy() returns, and after
main() returns. See ccol_mempool_destroy(3).

The library calls the functions of a table from the thread that does
the operation, so a table for a thread-safe object must hold
thread-safe functions.
.fi
.SS "Timeouts and durations"
.nf
In the public API, each timeout, deadline, interval and duration is a
uint64_t count of microseconds, and the name of each such parameter or
field ends in _us. No public signature takes a struct timespec, a
time_t or a long count of time, so the width of time_t in an
application does not have to match the width in the library.

The library changes a timeout into an absolute deadline on
CLOCK_MONOTONIC, so a change of the wall clock never makes a wait
longer or shorter. The arithmetic uses 64 bits on all targets and
saturates: a value that is too large for a deadline, for example
UINT64_MAX, becomes the latest deadline that the clock can hold, and
it never wraps into a deadline in the past. Where a system call takes
a larger unit of time, the library rounds the remaining time up, so a
wait never stops before its deadline.

For the timed calls of cthreadcomm and cthreadpool, a timeout of 0 does
not wait: the call does exactly what its try_ variant does and gives
the result of that variant, for example ccol_container_full or
ccol_container_empty, and never ccol_timed_out. The untimed call waits
without a limit. For all the other fields, the page of the field tells
what 0 means: no limit, or the default.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
To give a compile-time switch to the build of the library, use
EXTRA_CFLAGS, for example: make EXTRA_CFLAGS="-DNAME=VALUE".

CCOL_FORK_SAFETY_REQUIRED (default 1)
    With 1, the build includes the pthread_atfork() handlers of
    cthreadpool, cthreadcomm, clogger, chttpserver and chttpclient,
    which prevent a fork() from giving the child one of their internal
    locks in a locked state. With 0, the build leaves them out, and no
    fork() of the process pays their cost; the setting has no other
    effect on thread safety. With 0, the caller must not call fork()
    while a different thread can hold one of those locks. A program
    that never calls fork() is safe with either setting, and so is a
    program that calls fork() only in the two supported patterns
    below.

CCOL_MEMPOOL_COMPACT_LAYOUT (default 0)
    Selects the compact entry layout of cmempool. A translation unit
    that declares a buffer with
    CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER(3) must use the same
    setting as the library; otherwise the link fails with an undefined
    reference. See that page.

CCOL_MEMPOOL_DYNAMIC_TLS
    With 1, the per-thread fast paths of the library use the general
    thread-local storage model, so dlopen(3) of the library always
    succeeds. With 0, they use the initial-exec model. When the build
    does not set it, it is 0 with glibc and on FreeBSD, and 1 on all
    other systems. It changes no interface.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
The library supports only two ways to use fork(2) with a cthreadpool,
cthreadcomm, clogger, chttpserver or chttpclient handle:

  1. Call fork() before you create the handle, so that each process
     creates its own handles.
  2. Create the handle, then call fork() and immediately call exec(3)
     in the child.

The library does not support a child that continues without exec()
while a handle from before the fork() is live. fork() does not copy
the threads that these handles need, so a handle from the parent
cannot do work in the child, and you cannot make it operate again
there.

Each descriptor that the library creates is closed on exec, so a
child that calls exec() keeps none of them. On macOS, a socket, an
accepted socket and a pipe take two calls to become closed on exec,
and a fork() of another thread waits until both calls are done, which
takes some microseconds. posix_spawn(3) runs no fork handler, so it
does not wait. On macOS, a program that calls posix_spawn() while
another thread can create a socket through the library gives the
POSIX_SPAWN_CLOEXEC_DEFAULT flag to posix_spawnattr_setflags(3), and
names with posix_spawn_file_actions_addinherit_np(3) each descriptor
that the child must keep. This wait does not depend on
CCOL_FORK_SAFETY_REQUIRED.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
A component that moves data between threads, or that gives a shared
service, has its own synchronisation; a component for data work in one
thread has none.

No internal locks (put a lock around a shared instance yourself):
  cvector, chashmap, cbstmap, cstring, cjson, cyaml

Internal synchronisation (several threads can call at the same time):
  ccol_mempool, ccol_r_mempool (unless created single-threaded),
  ccol_circular_queue, ccol_dynamic_queue, ccol_channel,
  ccol_event_loop, clrucache, clogger, cthreadpool, chttpclient,
  chttpserver

Independent objects of a module without locks share no state, so two
threads can each use their own object. The pages of the synchronised
modules give the few calls that a caller must serialise, for example
two destroys of one handle.

The pthread wrappers of common.h (ccol_mutex_t and the others) are for
the portability of the library itself and are not part of the API. Use
pthreads or C11 <threads.h> to protect your own data.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

int main(void) {
  chmap_construct(ports, char *, int);
  chmap_insert(ports, "http", 80);

  const char *name = "gopher";
  cmap_pair key = { (void *)name, strlen(name) + 1 };
  int port;
  ccol_retval_t rv = chmap_get_elem_copy(ports, &key, &port,
                                         sizeof(port));
  if (rv != ccol_success)
    printf("%s: %s\en", name, ccol_retval_to_str(rv));

  chmap_destroy(ports);
  return 0;
}
.fi
